- **`pyproject.toml` lists what the code imports, both ways.** `rotproof check` compares the manifest at the root of
  each unit (the root of the project when it declares none) with the imports of the layers, the entry file and the
  tests (`tests/`), and installs nothing: it fails on an import of a package, or of another unit by the name its own
  manifest gives (`[project] name`), that the manifest does not list, and on a runtime dependency (`[project]
  dependencies`) that no code imports. The code of a layer uses runtime dependencies only, and a test uses those and the
  development ones (`[dependency-groups]`), which are never judged unused: put `pytest`, `ruff` and the like there. Names
  compare as PEP 503 says. An import is of the distribution named like it; when it is another (`dotenv` is
  `python-dotenv`), write `dotenv = "python-dotenv"` under `imports` in `[dependencies]` of `.config/rotproof.toml`, and
  list under `without_import` there a dependency the code uses without importing it (`openpyxl`, which `pandas` loads).
  The message says which, and names the distribution the environment in `.venv` has when there is one, which only words
  the hint: the verdict is the same on every machine. Fix a missing dependency by listing it in `pyproject.toml`, and an
  unused one by taking it out; never list a package only to pass the check.
- **A Python project is locked, and keeps one virtual environment.** `rotproof check` fails without a `pyproject.toml` at
  the root of each unit (a `requirements.txt` is no declaration), and without a lock file in that directory or above it
  (`uv.lock`, `poetry.lock`, `pdm.lock` or `pylock.toml`; a lock file the `.gitignore` excludes is not committed). Rotproof
  is a development dependency of the project, pinned in `dev` of `[dependency-groups]` and installed from the lock: raise
  it there, lock again with the tool of the project, then run `rotproof init`. A directory that holds a `pyvenv.cfg` is a
  virtual environment, found even where the `.gitignore` hides it, in any directory of the project (never below an ignored
  or hidden one). The only one allowed is `.venv`, in the directory of a lock file and ignored by the `.gitignore`: delete
  `venv/` and any other, and never make a second one in a package of a workspace that has one lock file at its root. Run
  the tools as they are (`uv sync`, `pdm install`, `poetry install` with `virtualenvs.in-project` set), so that they make
  `.venv`.
